<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Thank&nbsp;you&nbsp;again&nbsp;for&nbsp;the&nbsp;help</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Really my issue with this as a SCP is that 'the Foundation discovers the anomaly' feels too much like the end of the story than the start, and I'm kinda feeling like the history of the anomaly is the most interesting part? <strong>On the flip side, a deep, historical look at this could be very interesting if like, all the investigation is laid out; with the foundation assuming she's a new anomaly and then gradually piecing stuff together to the final ending where they discover who she was originally, y'know?</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>You and I are in agreement in how this narrative would/should take place, but I think it has merit as an article, with an accompanying tale.</p>
<p>The history of the anomaly is my prime focus. I've never characterized myself as a salesman, hence why an &quot;elevator pitch&quot; makes me nervous, but history is something I have a deep interest in and have dedicated my professional life to cataloguing and teaching. Perhaps I focused on the wrong aspect, as I am quick to discount people's liking of history outside of my own, and instead focused on the anomaly itself.</p>
<p>The extent of the article (still in brief) would be chronicling this SCP's existence over several hundred years, beginning with her discovery by the covenant. I was thinking that an accompanying chronicle of this covenant would be a good vehicle for detailing her existence, but this would be located <em>after the fact</em> and not immediately. The history between the Mekhanites and Sarkics is quite populated in ancient history and recent history, but the actual battles and struggle between them seems a little drowned out by the horrifying aspects of their dogma. This is an opportunity to show how their dogma and resulting rivalry produced a corruption perverse in the eyes of either side, and how everyone loses when they come to blows.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm not really sure what your addenda show, but I'm not convinced a test log would be that interesting here at all. I guess really what I want to know is how the narrative and the page layout tie together?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My thought on this, more explicitly, is:</p>
<p>I think there is a good way to piece this together and make it a compelling article, instead of a straightforward story which I think would allow for less intrigue. I've also considered the idea of making it so that the retrieval was an arduous process, and aspects of her were discovered during retrieval over a long period as she was tracked by a team. This would make it so that the article itself would detail bits and pieces of this entity which has a strange series of augmentations and behaviors, unravel bits of her further in the retrieval log, then tie it all together with the chronology (which would be incomplete/damaged) at the end of the article. Perhaps as well, a debate on whether to destroy such an item, exploit it, or keep it safe and hidden among different members of staff assigned to her.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

